666\" width=\"23.71514423076923\" fill=\"#333333\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(134.05341346153844 365.84) rotate(-40)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">14&deg;C</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"171.5637019230769\" y=\"221\" height=\"125\" width=\"23.71514423076923\" fill=\"#B3B3B3\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"195.27884615384613\" y=\"148.08333333333334\" height=\"197.91666666666666\" width=\"23.71514423076923\" fill=\"#333333\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(205.19884615384612 365.84) rotate(-40)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">16&deg;C</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"242.70913461538458\" y=\"231.41666666666669\" height=\"114.58333333333333\" width=\"23.71514423076923\" fill=\"#B3B3B3\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"266.4242788461538\" y=\"168.91666666666669\" height=\"177.08333333333331\" width=\"23.71514423076923\" fill=\"#333333\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(276.3442788461538 365.84) rotate(-40)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">18&deg;C</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"313.85456730769226\" y=\"262.6666666666667\" height=\"83.33333333333333\" width=\"23.71514423076923\" fill=\"#B3B3B3\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"337.5697115384615\" y=\"231.41666666666669\" height=\"114.58333333333333\" width=\"23.71514423076923\" fill=\"#333333\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(347.4897115384615 365.84) rotate(-40)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">20&deg;C</text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(230.8515625 24)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">Banana Ripening Time at Different </text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(230.8515625 48)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">Temperatures with and without </text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(230.8515625 72)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">Ethylene Treatment</text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(230.8515625 423.469150390625)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">Temperature (degrees Celsius)</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" fill=\"none\" x=\"116.31851959228516\" y=\"440.629150390625\" height=\"26.84\" width=\"229.0660858154297\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"123.31851959228516\" y=\"447.629150390625\" height=\"12\" width=\"12\" fill=\"#B3B3B3\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text transform=\"translate(142.31851959228516 459.629150390625)\" text-anchor=\"top\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">ethylene</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"221.93219757080078\" y=\"447.629150390625\" height=\"12\" width=\"12\" fill=\"#333333\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text transform=\"translate(240.93219757080078 459.629150390625)\" text-anchor=\"top\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">no ethylene</text></g></svg></figure><div class=\"sr-only\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for bar graph titled Banana Ripening Time at Different Temperatures with and without Ethylene Treatment\"><ul>\n<li>For each data category, the following bars are shown:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>ethylene</li>\n<li>no ethylene</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>All data are approximate.</li>\n<li>The Time data for the 4 categories are as follows:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>14 degrees Celsius:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>ethylene: 8 days</li>\n<li>no ethylene: 11 days</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>16 degrees Celsius:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>ethylene: 6 days</li>\n<li>no ethylene: 9.5 days</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>18 degrees Celsius:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>ethylene: 5.5 days</li>\n<li>no ethylene: 8.5 days</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>20 degrees Celsius:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>ethylene: 4 days</li>\n<li>no ethylene: 5.5 days</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p>A student is conducting an experiment to test the effect of temperature and ethylene treatment on the ripening speed of bananas. The student treated some bananas with ethylene while leaving others untreated, then allowed both types of bananas to ripen at one of four different temperatures. Comparing the data for bananas with and without ethylene, the student concluded that <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the student&rsquo;s conclusion?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7a90092c-024f-4d77-a615-ed3ee1cd74ac","content":"<p>20&deg;C is the ideal temperature at which to store bananas to slow ripening time.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"ba9e0108-1bb5-4365-b96f-6672003203e8","content":"<p>for those bananas that were not treated with ethylene, differences in temperature were not associated with absolute differences in ripening time.</p>"},{"id":"56aee830-7d3b-41a3-812e-c44fd9cc6d9f","content":"<p>bananas treated with ethylene ripen faster at 14&deg;C and 16&deg;C than at 18&deg;C and 20&deg;C.</p>"},{"id":"c2c55a14-b682-405c-9f78-0d7d7e55b146","content":"<p>ethylene was associated with a greater absolute change in ripening time at 14&deg;C, 16&deg;C, and 18&deg;C than at 20&deg;C.</p>"}],"keys":["c2c55a14-b682-405c-9f78-0d7d7e55b146"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the student&rsquo;s conclusion about banana ripening time with and without ethylene at different temperatures. The graph shows that at 20&deg;C, the gap between the two bars showing ripening times for ethylene-treated bananas and untreated bananas crosses fewer than 2 gridlines (from about 4 days for ethylene-treated bananas to about 5.5 days for untreated bananas). Meanwhile, the graph shows that at 14&deg;C, 16&deg;C, and 18&deg;C, the gap between the bars crosses more than 2 gridlines (from about 8 days for treated bananas to about 11 days for untreated bananas at 14&deg;C; from about 6 days for treated bananas to about 9.5 days for untreated bananas at 16&deg;C; and from about 5.5 days for treated bananas to about 8.5 days for untreated bananas at 18&deg;C). Since the gap between the bars at each of these temperatures crosses more than 2 gridlines, and since each of these gaps is larger than the gap between the bars at 20&deg;C, it can be concluded that ethylene was associated with a greater absolute change in ripening time at 14&deg;C, 16&deg;C, and 18&deg;C  than at 20&deg;C.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the graph shows that ethylene-treated bananas stored at 20&deg;C ripen more quickly than ethylene-treated bananas stored at the other temperatures do (about 4 days at 20&deg;C vs. about 5.5, 6, and 8 days at 18&deg;C, 16&deg;C, and 14&deg;C, respectively) and that untreated bananas stored at 20&deg;C ripen more quickly than untreated bananas stored at the other temperatures do (about 5.5 days at 20&deg;C vs. about 8.5, 9.5, and 11 days at 18&deg;C, 16&deg;C, and 14&deg;C, respectively). The information in the graph therefore indicates that storing bananas at 20&deg;C speeds up ripening time relative to storing bananas at the other temperatures shown, not that this storage temperature slows ripening time. Choice B is incorrect because the graph shows that as temperature increases, the ripening time of untreated bananas decreases, from about 11 days at 14&deg;C to about 5.5 days at 20&deg;C, with no exceptions to this trend. The graph therefore shows that differences in temperature were associated with absolute differences in ripening time, not that there was no association between differences in temperature and differences in ripening time. Choice C is incorrect because the graph shows that ripening times of ethylene-treated bananas at 14&deg;C and 16&deg;C were about 8 and 6 days, respectively, which is greater than, not less than, ripening times of ethylene-treated bananas at 18&deg;C and 20&deg;C, which were about 5.5 and 4 days, respectively. In other words, bananas treated with ethylene ripen more slowly, not faster, at 14&deg;C and 16&deg;C than at 18&deg;C and 20&deg;C.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061192,"updateDate":1755117061192}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061192, 1755117061192),
    ($SATQ$04bcb7a9$SATQ$, $SATQ$ddcb39de-3d58-4bc6-a2a2-0acfe8437055$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$70f7b390-f24a-425b-925e-490a866e9cef$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Xin Wang and colleagues have discovered the earliest known example of a flower bud in a 164-million-year-old plant fossil in China. The researchers have named the new species <em>Florigerminis jurassica. </em>They believe that the discovery pushes the emergence of flowering plants, or angiosperms, back to the Jurassic period, which occurred between 145 million and 201 million years ago.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>According to the text, how old was the fossil that Wang and colleagues discovered?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ef320bdf-412c-4e76-ae68-397d46457de1","content":"<p>150 million years old</p>"},{"id":"07f9cedf-45f2-416a-9da0-d14d08321ed8","content":"<p>145 million years old</p>"},{"id":"014cc17f-7a4e-46b5-8851-713494c6e198","content":"<p>164 million years old</p>"},{"id":"af5c4eba-bed6-4abe-83b2-f1c67b18d2f9","content":"<p>201 million years old</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["014cc17f-7a4e-46b5-8851-713494c6e198"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it gives the age for the fossil discovered by Wang and colleagues that is directly supported by the text. According to the text, Xin Wang and colleagues discovered a 164-million-year-old plant fossil. This plant fossil included a flower bud, which the researchers believe provides evidence that flowering plants emerged in the Jurassic period, which falls between 145 million and 201 million years ago. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text states that Wang and colleagues discovered a 164-million-year-old flowering plant fossil in China, not one that is 150 million years old. Although 150 million years ago would fall within the Jurassic period, according to the text it isn&rsquo;t the age of the discovered fossil. Choice B is incorrect because the text states that Wang and colleagues discovered a 164-million-year-old flowering plant fossil in China, not one that is 145 million years old. Although 145 million years ago would fall at the end of the Jurassic period, according to the text it isn&rsquo;t the age of the discovered fossil. Choice D is incorrect because the text states that Wang and colleagues discovered a 164-million-year-old flowering plant fossil in China, not one that is 201 million years old. Although 201 million years ago would fall at the beginning of the Jurassic period, according to the text it isn&rsquo;t the age of the discovered fossil. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-031-CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$f346a3c6-5dfa-4bdc-b107-8099c5576a7e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"04bcb7a9","external_id":"ddcb39de-3d58-4bc6-a2a2-0acfe8437055","vaultid":"70f7b390-f24a-425b-925e-490a866e9cef","uId":"7f20984b-2207-46b2-ad82-b9cac1b190fc","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Xin Wang and colleagues have discovered the earliest known example of a flower bud in a 164-million-year-old plant fossil in China. The researchers have named the new species <em>Florigerminis jurassica. </em>They believe that the discovery pushes the emergence of flowering plants, or angiosperms, back to the Jurassic period, which occurred between 145 million and 201 million years ago.</p>","stem":"<p>According to the text, how old was the fossil that Wang and colleagues discovered?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ef320bdf-412c-4e76-ae68-397d46457de1","content":"<p>150 million years old</p>"},{"id":"07f9cedf-45f2-416a-9da0-d14d08321ed8","content":"<p>145 million years old</p>"},{"id":"014cc17f-7a4e-46b5-8851-713494c6e198","content":"<p>164 million years old</p>"},{"id":"af5c4eba-bed6-4abe-83b2-f1c67b18d2f9","content":"<p>201 million years old</p>"}],"keys":["014cc17f-7a4e-46b5-8851-713494c6e198"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it gives the age for the fossil discovered by Wang and colleagues that is directly supported by the text. According to the text, Xin Wang and colleagues discovered a 164-million-year-old plant fossil. This plant fossil included a flower bud, which the researchers believe provides evidence that flowering plants emerged in the Jurassic period, which falls between 145 million and 201 million years ago. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text states that Wang and colleagues discovered a 164-million-year-old flowering plant fossil in China, not one that is 150 million years old. Although 150 million years ago would fall within the Jurassic period, according to the text it isn&rsquo;t the age of the discovered fossil. Choice B is incorrect because the text states that Wang and colleagues discovered a 164-million-year-old flowering plant fossil in China, not one that is 145 million years old. Although 145 million years ago would fall at the end of the Jurassic period, according to the text it isn&rsquo;t the age of the discovered fossil. Choice D is incorrect because the text states that Wang and colleagues discovered a 164-million-year-old flowering plant fossil in China, not one that is 201 million years old. Although 201 million years ago would fall at the beginning of the Jurassic period, according to the text it isn&rsquo;t the age of the discovered fossil. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-031-CID","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"f346a3c6-5dfa-4bdc-b107-8099c5576a7e","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959319,"updateDate":1691007959319}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959319, 1691007959319),
    ($SATQ$04cbeca3$SATQ$, $SATQ$95a5360b-3135-4780-921f-c511f0ae2578$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$12087eab-073b-4e59-84f3-d0b331d866a1$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 1534 CE, King Henry VIII of England split with the Catholic Church and declared himself head of the Church of England, in part because Pope Clement VII refused to annul his marriage to Catherine of Aragon. Two years later, Henry VIII introduced a policy titled the Dissolution of the Monasteries that by 1540 had resulted in the closure of all Catholic monasteries in England and the confiscation of their estates. Some historians assert that the enactment of the policy was primarily motivated by perceived financial opportunities.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which quotation from a scholarly article best supports the assertion of the historians mentioned in the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0665cb1f-f77c-47cd-8829-88adacaa3b80","content":"<p>&ldquo;At the time of the Dissolution of the Monasteries, about 2 percent of the adult male population of England were monks; by 1690, the proportion of the adult male population who were monks was less than 1 percent.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"75806f5c-13a1-4543-8a78-26267b3d6679","content":"<p>&ldquo;A contemporary description of the Dissolution of the Monasteries, Michael Sherbrook&rsquo;s <em>Falle of the Religious Howses</em>, recounts witness testimony that monks were allowed to keep the contents of their cells and that the monastery timber was purchased by local yeomen.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"7f59eab0-4e4e-4aec-a9d6-5631439a0ed1","content":"<p>&ldquo;In 1535, the year before enacting the Dissolution of the Monasteries, Henry commissioned a survey of the value of church holdings in England&mdash;the work, performed by sheriffs, bishops, and magistrates, began that January and was swiftly completed by the summer.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"7bd0fd9f-d2e6-4abe-85a6-bbf03fe7c4c2","content":"<p>&ldquo;The October 1536 revolt known as the Pilgrimage of Grace had several economic motives: high food prices due to a poor harvest the prior year; the Dissolution of the Monasteries, which closed reliable sources of food and shelter for many; and rents and taxes throughout Northern England that were not merely high but predatory.&rdquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7f59eab0-4e4e-4aec-a9d6-5631439a0ed1"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The fact that Henry VIII commissioned a survey of church holdings just before enacting the Dissolution of the Monasteries suggests that he was interested in the potential profits from claiming their assets. This supports the historians&rsquo; assertion. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice describes a potential effect of the Dissolution of the Monasteries. The decrease in the proportion of monks in England isn&rsquo;t relevant to the question of Henry VIII&rsquo;s motivations. Choice B is incorrect. Details about how the monks were treated during the dissolution don&rsquo;t address Henry VIII&rsquo;s motivations for enacting the policy in the first place. That the monks could keep the content of their cells (their rooms) and sell off the timber they&rsquo;d harvested has no impact on the value of the monasteries&rsquo; estates&mdash;their land holdings. Choice D is incorrect. This choice mentions one impact that the Dissolution of the Monasteries contributed to, two years after it happened. But it doesn&rsquo;t help explain why Henry VIII might have wanted to enact the policy in the first place. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TXT-H007$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$ad9599bd-6332-4f03-b287-cdb26f189154$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"04cbeca3","external_id":"95a5360b-3135-4780-921f-c511f0ae2578","vaultid":"12087eab-073b-4e59-84f3-d0b331d866a1","uId":"17f087dc-c5c8-4e17-a591-099f700c180d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 1534 CE, King Henry VIII of England split with the Catholic Church and declared himself head of the Church of England, in part because Pope Clement VII refused to annul his marriage to Catherine of Aragon. Two years later, Henry VIII introduced a policy titled the Dissolution of the Monasteries that by 1540 had resulted in the closure of all Catholic monasteries in England and the confiscation of their estates. Some historians assert that the enactment of the policy was primarily motivated by perceived financial opportunities.</p>","stem":"<p>Which quotation from a scholarly article best supports the assertion of the historians mentioned in the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0665cb1f-f77c-47cd-8829-88adacaa3b80","content":"<p>&ldquo;At the time of the Dissolution of the Monasteries, about 2 percent of the adult male population of England were monks; by 1690, the proportion of the adult male population who were monks was less than 1 percent.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"75806f5c-13a1-4543-8a78-26267b3d6679","content":"<p>&ldquo;A contemporary description of the Dissolution of the Monasteries, Michael Sherbrook&rsquo;s <em>Falle of the Religious Howses</em>, recounts witness testimony that monks were allowed to keep the contents of their cells and that the monastery timber was purchased by local yeomen.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"7f59eab0-4e4e-4aec-a9d6-5631439a0ed1","content":"<p>&ldquo;In 1535, the year before enacting the Dissolution of the Monasteries, Henry commissioned a survey of the value of church holdings in England&mdash;the work, performed by sheriffs, bishops, and magistrates, began that January and was swiftly completed by the summer.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"7bd0fd9f-d2e6-4abe-85a6-bbf03fe7c4c2","content":"<p>&ldquo;The October 1536 revolt known as the Pilgrimage of Grace had several economic motives: high food prices due to a poor harvest the prior year; the Dissolution of the Monasteries, which closed reliable sources of food and shelter for many; and rents and taxes throughout Northern England that were not merely high but predatory.&rdquo;</p>"}],"keys":["7f59eab0-4e4e-4aec-a9d6-5631439a0ed1"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The fact that Henry VIII commissioned a survey of church holdings just before enacting the Dissolution of the Monasteries suggests that he was interested in the potential profits from claiming their assets. This supports the historians&rsquo; assertion. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice describes a potential effect of the Dissolution of the Monasteries. The decrease in the proportion of monks in England isn&rsquo;t relevant to the question of Henry VIII&rsquo;s motivations. Choice B is incorrect. Details about how the monks were treated during the dissolution don&rsquo;t address Henry VIII&rsquo;s motivations for enacting the policy in the first place. That the monks could keep the content of their cells (their rooms) and sell off the timber they&rsquo;d harvested has no impact on the value of the monasteries&rsquo; estates&mdash;their land holdings. Choice D is incorrect. This choice mentions one impact that the Dissolution of the Monasteries contributed to, two years after it happened. But it doesn&rsquo;t help explain why Henry VIII might have wanted to enact the policy in the first place. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TXT-H007","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"ad9599bd-6332-4f03-b287-cdb26f189154","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959376,"updateDate":1691007959376}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959376, 1691007959376),
    ($SATQ$04dff083$SATQ$, $SATQ$73bf8fb7-a095-496b-b9f5-109b6af2d221$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$CID$SATQ$, $SATQ$Central Ideas and Details$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In present-day Chiapas, Mexico, archaeologist Robert Rosenswig, remote-sensing specialist Ricardo L&oacute;pez-Torrijos, and colleagues have located 41 smaller settlements surrounding the ancient Mesoamerican city of Izapa. The researchers have concluded that these settlements were culturally linked to Izapa because each of the settlements is the same age and configured in the same manner as Izapa, with a pyramid to the north and a plaza to the south. Their shared structural orientation suggests that residents of the settlements likely performed some of the same cultural ceremonies as residents in Izapa did.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"904f3c03-2b08-4e9c-89b8-6672699508bf","content":"<p>Researchers have determined that the arrangement of Izapa&rsquo;s structures was based on those of other nearby settlements.</p>"},{"id":"f1349e0d-ad50-45db-841f-2eaa6f1cc4cc","content":"<p>Cultural ceremonies in Izapa seem to have played a more important role for its residents than those in smaller, surrounding settlements did.</p>"},{"id":"a7629da4-19aa-4338-8f55-197ee8aa9875","content":"<p>Although archaeologists have learned much about Izapa over years of research, they have only recently found the smaller settlements that surrounded it.</p>"},{"id":"c68fcebf-873d-40a3-8a0e-7f9af41740bc","content":"<p>Researchers have inferred that Izapa was related to the smaller settlements that surrounded it based in part on the similarity of their construction.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c68fcebf-873d-40a3-8a0e-7f9af41740bc"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately states the main idea of the text. The text indicates that Rosenswig, L&oacute;pez-Torrijos, and colleagues concluded that there is a cultural link between the city of Izapa and the 41 smaller settlements they located around the city. According to the text, the researchers reached this conclusion because Izapa and the settlements are of the same age and configuration in that all have a pyramid to the north and a plaza to the south. Thus, the main idea of the text is that researchers have inferred that Izapa was related to the smaller settlements that surrounded it based in part on the similarity of their construction.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text discusses a similarity between how structures in Izapa and nearby settlements are arranged, the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the arrangement in Izapa is based on those of the nearby settlements. Choice B is incorrect. Although the text mentions that residents of Izapa and its surrounding settlements may have performed the same cultural ceremonies, the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss the ceremonies&rsquo; importance for the residents. Choice C is incorrect. Although the text describes findings about Izapa, it doesn&rsquo;t specify how much archaeologists have learned about Izapa over the years. Additionally, while the text indicates that the discovery of the 41 smaller settlements surrounding Izapa was made in present-day Chiapas and thus may be of recent date, this is a detail in the text, not its main idea.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"04dff083","external_id":"73bf8fb7-a095-496b-b9f5-109b6af2d221","vaultid":null,"uId":"6321dbfb-f2e3-42a2-9a8c-d8f229b87838","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"CID","skill_desc":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In present-day Chiapas, Mexico, archaeologist Robert Rosenswig, remote-sensing specialist Ricardo L&oacute;pez-Torrijos, and colleagues have located 41 smaller settlements surrounding the ancient Mesoamerican city of Izapa. The researchers have concluded that these settlements were culturally linked to Izapa because each of the settlements is the same age and configured in the same manner as Izapa, with a pyramid to the north and a plaza to the south. Their shared structural orientation suggests that residents of the settlements likely performed some of the same cultural ceremonies as residents in Izapa did.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice best states the main idea of the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"904f3c03-2b08-4e9c-89b8-6672699508bf","content":"<p>Researchers have determined that the arrangement of Izapa&rsquo;s structures was based on those of other nearby settlements.</p>"},{"id":"f1349e0d-ad50-45db-841f-2eaa6f1cc4cc","content":"<p>Cultural ceremonies in Izapa seem to have played a more important role for its residents than those in smaller, surrounding settlements did.</p>"},{"id":"a7629da4-19aa-4338-8f55-197ee8aa9875","content":"<p>Although archaeologists have learned much about Izapa over years of research, they have only recently found the smaller settlements that surrounded it.</p>"},{"id":"c68fcebf-873d-40a3-8a0e-7f9af41740bc","content":"<p>Researchers have inferred that Izapa was related to the smaller settlements that surrounded it based in part on the similarity of their construction.</p>"}],"keys":["c68fcebf-873d-40a3-8a0e-7f9af41740bc"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most accurately states the main idea of the text. The text indicates that Rosenswig, L&oacute;pez-Torrijos, and colleagues concluded that there is a cultural link between the city of Izapa and the 41 smaller settlements they located around the city. According to the text, the researchers reached this conclusion because Izapa and the settlements are of the same age and configuration in that all have a pyramid to the north and a plaza to the south. Thus, the main idea of the text is that researchers have inferred that Izapa was related to the smaller settlements that surrounded it based in part on the similarity of their construction.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. Although the text discusses a similarity between how structures in Izapa and nearby settlements are arranged, the text doesn&rsquo;t indicate that the arrangement in Izapa is based on those of the nearby settlements. Choice B is incorrect. Although the text mentions that residents of Izapa and its surrounding settlements may have performed the same cultural ceremonies, the text doesn&rsquo;t discuss the ceremonies&rsquo; importance for the residents. Choice C is incorrect. Although the text describes findings about Izapa, it doesn&rsquo;t specify how much archaeologists have learned about Izapa over the years. Additionally, while the text indicates that the discovery of the 41 smaller settlements surrounding Izapa was made in present-day Chiapas and thus may be of recent date, this is a detail in the text, not its main idea.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323041,"updateDate":1730147323041}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323041, 1730147323041),
    ($SATQ$068f939b$SATQ$, $SATQ$c776357f-bef8-451f-ad4f-fcec70402ee7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The ancient Greek concept of &ldquo;mimesis,&rdquo; a term used in the works of Plato, Aristotle, and other Greek philosophers in discussions of representational art&mdash;visual, performance, or literary art that aims to depict the real world&mdash;is a foundational concept of the Western philosophy of aesthetics. Mimesis is typically translated as &ldquo;imitation&rdquo; in modern editions of ancient Greek texts, but scholar Stephen Halliwell warns that this is overly reductive: &ldquo;imitation&rdquo; implies that art merely copies&mdash;and is thus by definition entirely derivative of&mdash;a reality that exists outside and prior to the work of art, and translating &ldquo;mimesis&rdquo; thusly obscures the multifaceted ways in which the ancient Greeks understood the relationship between art and reality.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which statement, if true, would most directly support the claim by Halliwell presented in the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"fcb99257-4c43-4b2a-8744-5c3896323540","content":"<p>One of the earliest appearances of mimesis&rsquo;s root word, <em>mimos</em>, can be found in an ancient Greek tragedy in reference to dramatic impersonation, and the <em>mim-</em> root came to be generally associated with the musical and poetic arts by the fifth century BCE.</p>"},{"id":"5f336128-e4ab-49e3-80ae-59cb588088bb","content":"<p>Both Plato&rsquo;s and Aristotle&rsquo;s theorizations of mimesis examine the psychological effects that works of art induce in the viewer or listener.</p>"},{"id":"951ec032-d16c-44dc-8069-0da7c8610de2","content":"<p>Although several of Plato&rsquo;s earliest philosophical works discuss aesthetic ideas, the term &ldquo;mimesis&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t appear in Plato&rsquo;s discussions of art until <em>Cratylus</em>, a relatively late work.</p>"},{"id":"0dd23e44-2640-4cc3-af77-d10ed0bfd715","content":"<p>Although Plato&rsquo;s writings typically characterize representational art as an inferior reflection of the physical world, Aristotle suggests that mimesis can refer to art&rsquo;s capacity to envision hypothetical conditions that could, but don&rsquo;t yet, exist.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0dd23e44-2640-4cc3-af77-d10ed0bfd715"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it presents a statement that would support Halliwell&rsquo;s claim that "imitation" is an overly simplified translation of the word "mimesis" in the context of ancient Greek philosophical discussions of representational art and that, because it suggests a view of art as merely copying things that exist in reality, the translation obscures the fact that Plato, Aristotle, and other ancient Greeks understood the relationship between art and reality in varied ways. If Plato&rsquo;s works tend to treat representational art as an inferior or inadequate reflection of the physical world (consistent with the idea of mere imitation), while Aristotle&rsquo;s works suggest that the term "mimesis" can refer to art&rsquo;s ability to show conditions that don&rsquo;t currently exist but could exist (going beyond imitation into novel creation), that evidence would support the claim that the term "mimesis" means more than just "imitation" and that ancient Greek philosophers held different views of the relationship between art and reality.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because information about the root word <em>mimos</em> first being used in drama and the root <em>mim</em>- coming to be associated with music and poetry wouldn&rsquo;t indicate anything about the meaning of the specific term "mimesis" as it was used by ancient Greek philosophers in discussions of representational art; thus, the information would have no bearing on Halliwell&rsquo;s claim that a common translation of the specific term is an oversimplification. Choice B is incorrect because the issue of art&rsquo;s psychological effects on audiences gets at how people respond to works of art instead of how art itself is related to reality, so the idea that Plato and Aristotle both addressed such effects in their considerations of mimesis wouldn&rsquo;t have any bearing on Halliwell&rsquo;s claim that a common translation of that term oversimplifies the relationship between art and reality. Further, this idea would touch on one general similarity in approaches to mimesis instead of illustrating the multifaceted, or varied, ways Halliwell claims ancient Greek philosophers understood art&rsquo;s relation to reality. Choice C is incorrect because the idea that Plato discussed aesthetics in his early works but didn&rsquo;t use the term "mimesis" until later doesn&rsquo;t give any indication of how Plato or any other ancient Greek philosophers understood the relation between art and reality, so it would have no bearing on Halliwell&rsquo;s claim that a common translation of the term "mimesis" is oversimplified and fails to reflect the varied ways the philosophers understood that relationship.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"068f939b","external_id":"c776357f-bef8-451f-ad4f-fcec70402ee7","vaultid":null,"uId":"09e513a4-4b09-400e-9789-a83a059e5c4b","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The ancient Greek concept of &ldquo;mimesis,&rdquo; a term used in the works of Plato, Aristotle, and other Greek philosophers in discussions of representational art&mdash;visual, performance, or literary art that aims to depict the real world&mdash;is a foundational concept of the Western philosophy of aesthetics. Mimesis is typically translated as &ldquo;imitation&rdquo; in modern editions of ancient Greek texts, but scholar Stephen Halliwell warns that this is overly reductive: &ldquo;imitation&rdquo; implies that art merely copies&mdash;and is thus by definition entirely derivative of&mdash;a reality that exists outside and prior to the work of art, and translating &ldquo;mimesis&rdquo; thusly obscures the multifaceted ways in which the ancient Greeks understood the relationship between art and reality.</p>","stem":"<p>Which statement, if true, would most directly support the claim by Halliwell presented in the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"fcb99257-4c43-4b2a-8744-5c3896323540","content":"<p>One of the earliest appearances of mimesis&rsquo;s root word, <em>mimos</em>, can be found in an ancient Greek tragedy in reference to dramatic impersonation, and the <em>mim-</em> root came to be generally associated with the musical and poetic arts by the fifth century BCE.</p>"},{"id":"5f336128-e4ab-49e3-80ae-59cb588088bb","content":"<p>Both Plato&rsquo;s and Aristotle&rsquo;s theorizations of mimesis examine the psychological effects that works of art induce in the viewer or listener.</p>"},{"id":"951ec032-d16c-44dc-8069-0da7c8610de2","content":"<p>Although several of Plato&rsquo;s earliest philosophical works discuss aesthetic ideas, the term &ldquo;mimesis&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t appear in Plato&rsquo;s discussions of art until <em>Cratylus</em>, a relatively late work.</p>"},{"id":"0dd23e44-2640-4cc3-af77-d10ed0bfd715","content":"<p>Although Plato&rsquo;s writings typically characterize representational art as an inferior reflection of the physical world, Aristotle suggests that mimesis can refer to art&rsquo;s capacity to envision hypothetical conditions that could, but don&rsquo;t yet, exist.</p>"}],"keys":["0dd23e44-2640-4cc3-af77-d10ed0bfd715"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it presents a statement that would support Halliwell&rsquo;s claim that \"imitation\" is an overly simplified translation of the word \"mimesis\" in the context of ancient Greek philosophical discussions of representational art and that, because it suggests a view of art as merely copying things that exist in reality, the translation obscures the fact that Plato, Aristotle, and other ancient Greeks understood the relationship between art and reality in varied ways. If Plato&rsquo;s works tend to treat representational art as an inferior or inadequate reflection of the physical world (consistent with the idea of mere imitation), while Aristotle&rsquo;s works suggest that the term \"mimesis\" can refer to art&rsquo;s ability to show conditions that don&rsquo;t currently exist but could exist (going beyond imitation into novel creation), that evidence would support the claim that the term \"mimesis\" means more than just \"imitation\" and that ancient Greek philosophers held different views of the relationship between art and reality.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because information about the root word <em>mimos</em> first being used in drama and the root <em>mim</em>- coming to be associated with music and poetry wouldn&rsquo;t indicate anything about the meaning of the specific term \"mimesis\" as it was used by ancient Greek philosophers in discussions of representational art; thus, the information would have no bearing on Halliwell&rsquo;s claim that a common translation of the specific term is an oversimplification. Choice B is incorrect because the issue of art&rsquo;s psychological effects on audiences gets at how people respond to works of art instead of how art itself is related to reality, so the idea that Plato and Aristotle both addressed such effects in their considerations of mimesis wouldn&rsquo;t have any bearing on Halliwell&rsquo;s claim that a common translation of that term oversimplifies the relationship between art and reality. Further, this idea would touch on one general similarity in approaches to mimesis instead of illustrating the multifaceted, or varied, ways Halliwell claims ancient Greek philosophers understood art&rsquo;s relation to reality. Choice C is incorrect because the idea that Plato discussed aesthetics in his early works but didn&rsquo;t use the term \"mimesis\" until later doesn&rsquo;t give any indication of how Plato or any other ancient Greek philosophers understood the relation between art and reality, so it would have no bearing on Halliwell&rsquo;s claim that a common translation of the term \"mimesis\" is oversimplified and fails to reflect the varied ways the philosophers understood that relationship.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061191,"updateDate":1755117061191}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061191, 1755117061191),
    ($SATQ$0770b53d$SATQ$, $SATQ$a362b6b6-1fae-4fcc-a097-241ddb0addd2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$5f7a7ea8-82c4-4d13-aea4-8d7c1f814c46$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p><em>O Pioneers!</em> is a 1913 novel by Willa Cather. In the novel, Cather portrays Alexandra Bergson as having a deep emotional connection to her natural surroundings: <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which quotation from <em>O Pioneers!</em> most effectively illustrates the claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0c2422a8-d1cd-45e8-a1e5-7e79daa7d29e","content":"<p>&ldquo;She had never known before how much the country meant to her. The chirping of the insects down in the long grass had been like the sweetest music. She had felt as if her heart were hiding down there, somewhere, with the quail and the plover and all the little wild things that crooned or buzzed in the sun. Under the long shaggy ridges, she felt the future stirring.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"c5be5497-3a9f-436f-8981-779b1af149b1","content":"<p>&ldquo;Alexandra talked to the men about their crops and to the women about their poultry. She spent a whole day with one young farmer who had been away at school, and who was experimenting with a new kind of clover hay. She learned a great deal.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"89d3f21c-fb2e-4971-a29c-a8d2fdc15d55","content":"<p>&ldquo;Alexandra drove off alone. The rattle of her wagon was lost in the howling of the wind, but her lantern, held firmly between her feet, made a moving point of light along the highway, going deeper and deeper into the dark country.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"d6099f82-92ec-4936-bfb9-c67eb00ef738","content":"<p>&ldquo;It was Alexandra who read the papers and followed the markets, and who learned by the mistakes of their neighbors. It was Alexandra who could always tell about what it had cost to fatten each steer, and who could guess the weight of a hog before it went on the scales closer than John Bergson [her father] himself.&rdquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0c2422a8-d1cd-45e8-a1e5-7e79daa7d29e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it presents the quotation that most directly illustrates the claim that Cather portrays Alexandra as having a deep emotional connection to her natural surroundings. This quotation states that the country meant a great deal to Alexandra and then goes on to detail several ways in which her natural surroundings affect her emotionally: the insects sound like &ldquo;the sweetest music,&rdquo; she feels as though &ldquo;her heart were hiding&rdquo; in the grass &ldquo;with the quail and the plover,&rdquo; and near the ridges she feels &ldquo;the future stirring.&rdquo; </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the quotation doesn&rsquo;t suggest that Alexandra had a deep emotional connection to her natural surroundings but instead describes how she interacts with the people around her to learn more about crops, poultry, and experiments with clover hay. Choice C is incorrect because the quotation doesn&rsquo;t suggest that Alexandra has a deep emotional connection to her natural surroundings but instead describes her nighttime departure in a wagon. The quotation says nothing about Alexandra&rsquo;s emotional state. Choice D is incorrect because the quotation doesn&rsquo;t convey Alexandra&rsquo;s deep emotional connection to her natural surroundings; instead, this quotation describes how well she understands the markets and livestock.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$P89-T037$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$c627781d-ff39-4779-bab3-9b71fba9a72d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0770b53d","external_id":"a362b6b6-1fae-4fcc-a097-241ddb0addd2","vaultid":"5f7a7ea8-82c4-4d13-aea4-8d7c1f814c46","uId":"1709e144-4a1f-4e93-a25c-3768922ff17a","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p><em>O Pioneers!</em> is a 1913 novel by Willa Cather. In the novel, Cather portrays Alexandra Bergson as having a deep emotional connection to her natural surroundings: <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which quotation from <em>O Pioneers!</em> most effectively illustrates the claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0c2422a8-d1cd-45e8-a1e5-7e79daa7d29e","content":"<p>&ldquo;She had never known before how much the country meant to her. The chirping of the insects down in the long grass had been like the sweetest music. She had felt as if her heart were hiding down there, somewhere, with the quail and the plover and all the little wild things that crooned or buzzed in the sun. Under the long shaggy ridges, she felt the future stirring.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"c5be5497-3a9f-436f-8981-779b1af149b1","content":"<p>&ldquo;Alexandra talked to the men about their crops and to the women about their poultry. She spent a whole day with one young farmer who had been away at school, and who was experimenting with a new kind of clover hay. She learned a great deal.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"89d3f21c-fb2e-4971-a29c-a8d2fdc15d55","content":"<p>&ldquo;Alexandra drove off alone. The rattle of her wagon was lost in the howling of the wind, but her lantern, held firmly between her feet, made a moving point of light along the highway, going deeper and deeper into the dark country.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"d6099f82-92ec-4936-bfb9-c67eb00ef738","content":"<p>&ldquo;It was Alexandra who read the papers and followed the markets, and who learned by the mistakes of their neighbors. It was Alexandra who could always tell about what it had cost to fatten each steer, and who could guess the weight of a hog before it went on the scales closer than John Bergson [her father] himself.&rdquo;</p>"}],"keys":["0c2422a8-d1cd-45e8-a1e5-7e79daa7d29e"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it presents the quotation that most directly illustrates the claim that Cather portrays Alexandra as having a deep emotional connection to her natural surroundings. This quotation states that the country meant a great deal to Alexandra and then goes on to detail several ways in which her natural surroundings affect her emotionally: the insects sound like &ldquo;the sweetest music,&rdquo; she feels as though &ldquo;her heart were hiding&rdquo; in the grass &ldquo;with the quail and the plover,&rdquo; and near the ridges she feels &ldquo;the future stirring.&rdquo; </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the quotation doesn&rsquo;t suggest that Alexandra had a deep emotional connection to her natural surroundings but instead describes how she interacts with the people around her to learn more about crops, poultry, and experiments with clover hay. Choice C is incorrect because the quotation doesn&rsquo;t suggest that Alexandra has a deep emotional connection to her natural surroundings but instead describes her nighttime departure in a wagon. The quotation says nothing about Alexandra&rsquo;s emotional state. Choice D is incorrect because the quotation doesn&rsquo;t convey Alexandra&rsquo;s deep emotional connection to her natural surroundings; instead, this quotation describes how well she understands the markets and livestock.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"P89-T037","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c627781d-ff39-4779-bab3-9b71fba9a72d","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959378,"updateDate":1691007959378}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959378, 1691007959378),
    ($SATQ$08395130$SATQ$, $SATQ$9296e03c-58be-4531-9996-2ed70f7b4bac$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$154f81a8-0b97-4973-8651-95fba4cdd1f5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The Hubble Space Telescope (HST) is projected to maintain operation until at least 2030, but it has already revolutionized high-resolution imaging of solar-system bodies in visible and ultraviolet (UV) light wavelengths, notwithstanding that only about 6% of the bodies imaged by the HST are within the solar system. NASA researcher Cindy L. Young and colleagues assert that a new space telescope dedicated exclusively to solar-system observations would permit an extensive survey of minor solar-system bodies and long-term UV observation to discern how solar-system bodies change over time. Young and colleagues&rsquo; recommendation therefore implies that the HST <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6055a852-70f6-4312-b36a-d004f304594a","content":"<p>will likely continue to be used primarily to observe objects outside the solar system.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"3c91ab72-91db-4c0d-b5de-a4eb0de894f8","content":"<p>will no longer be used to observe solar system objects if the telescope recommended by Young and colleagues is deployed.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"9a8e0c17-4598-409d-a243-74da0160cb15","content":"<p>can be modified to observe the features of solar system objects that are of interest to Young and colleagues.</p>"},{"id":"d626d067-edbc-40e6-8082-935495fc0427","content":"<p>lacks the sensors to observe the wavelengths of light needed to discern how solar system bodies change over time.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6055a852-70f6-4312-b36a-d004f304594a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The HST will operate until at least 2030, but it&rsquo;s only observing stuff inside our solar system 6% of the time. If we could get a different telescope to observe stuff inside our solar system 100% of the time and take more extensive images of certain things, then the HST could continue to be used mainly for observing stuff outside the solar system. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This inference is too strong to be supported by the text. Even if the new telescope is deployed, the HST might still be used as it&rsquo;s being used now. Based on the text, the new telescope would just be used for more extensive and long-term imaging of solar system bodies, which doesn&rsquo;t necessarily overlap with the HST. Choice C is incorrect. This inference isn&rsquo;t supported. The text never mentions the possibility of modifying the HST, so there is no basis to make this inference. Rather, the researchers suggest using a different telescope to more closely observe certain objects. Choice D is incorrect. This inference is too strong to be supported. The text doesn&rsquo;t give us enough info to assume that the HST lacks any particular sensors. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-INF-H004$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$d291a178-c0d9-402c-b673-864970c20a4a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"08395130","external_id":"9296e03c-58be-4531-9996-2ed70f7b4bac","vaultid":"154f81a8-0b97-4973-8651-95fba4cdd1f5","uId":"4eb7da29-9215-42ab-b8ad-5222a8bec72d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The Hubble Space Telescope (HST) is projected to maintain operation until at least 2030, but it has already revolutionized high-resolution imaging of solar-system bodies in visible and ultraviolet (UV) light wavelengths, notwithstanding that only about 6% of the bodies imaged by the HST are within the solar system. NASA researcher Cindy L. Young and colleagues assert that a new space telescope dedicated exclusively to solar-system observations would permit an extensive survey of minor solar-system bodies and long-term UV observation to discern how solar-system bodies change over time. Young and colleagues&rsquo; recommendation therefore implies that the HST <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6055a852-70f6-4312-b36a-d004f304594a","content":"<p>will likely continue to be used primarily to observe objects outside the solar system.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"3c91ab72-91db-4c0d-b5de-a4eb0de894f8","content":"<p>will no longer be used to observe solar system objects if the telescope recommended by Young and colleagues is deployed.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"9a8e0c17-4598-409d-a243-74da0160cb15","content":"<p>can be modified to observe the features of solar system objects that are of interest to Young and colleagues.</p>"},{"id":"d626d067-edbc-40e6-8082-935495fc0427","content":"<p>lacks the sensors to observe the wavelengths of light needed to discern how solar system bodies change over time.</p>"}],"keys":["6055a852-70f6-4312-b36a-d004f304594a"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The HST will operate until at least 2030, but it&rsquo;s only observing stuff inside our solar system 6% of the time. If we could get a different telescope to observe stuff inside our solar system 100% of the time and take more extensive images of certain things, then the HST could continue to be used mainly for observing stuff outside the solar system. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This inference is too strong to be supported by the text. Even if the new telescope is deployed, the HST might still be used as it&rsquo;s being used now. Based on the text, the new telescope would just be used for more extensive and long-term imaging of solar system bodies, which doesn&rsquo;t necessarily overlap with the HST. Choice C is incorrect. This inference isn&rsquo;t supported. The text never mentions the possibility of modifying the HST, so there is no basis to make this inference. Rather, the researchers suggest using a different telescope to more closely observe certain objects. Choice D is incorrect. This inference is too strong to be supported. The text doesn&rsquo;t give us enough info to assume that the HST lacks any particular sensors. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-INF-H004","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"d291a178-c0d9-402c-b673-864970c20a4a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959368,"updateDate":1691007959368}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959368, 1691007959368),
    ($SATQ$086dd8cc$SATQ$, $SATQ$350515f3-0f56-40e1-b5ce-af122f093fe5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$INF$SATQ$, $SATQ$Inferences$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The morphological novelty of echinoderms&mdash;marine invertebrates with radial symmetry, usually starlike, around a central point&mdash;impedes comparisons with most other animals, in which bilateral symmetry on an anterior-posterior (head to tail) axis through a trunk is typical. Particularly puzzling are sea stars, thought to have evolved a headless layout from a known bilateral origin. Applying genomic knowledge of <em>Saccoglossus kowalevskii</em> acorn worms (close relatives of sea stars, and thus expected to have similar markers for corresponding anatomical regions) to the body patterning genes of <em>Patiria miniata</em> sea stars, Laurent Formery <span data-ssml-sub-alias="et al">et al.</span> observed activity only in anterior genes across <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias="P">P.</span> miniata</em>&rsquo;s entire body and some posterior genes limited to the edges, suggesting that <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4180cf85-c572-4011-a51e-49d8cde13e5f","content":"<p>despite the greater prevalence of anterior genes in sea stars&rsquo; genetic makeup, posterior genes active at the body&rsquo;s perimeter are primarily responsible for the starlike layout that distinguishes sea stars&rsquo; radial symmetry from that of other echinoderms.</p>"},{"id":"9d2012ba-875c-449e-a22f-ce98cd07e68d","content":"<p>contrary to the belief that they evolved from early ancestors with the bilateral form typical of many other animals, sea stars instead originated with an atypical body layout that was neither bilaterally nor radially symmetrical.</p>"},{"id":"883b1069-8080-4363-86fe-41574c1ce527","content":"<p>although the two species are closely related, there is only minimal correspondence in the genetic markers for head, tail, and trunk region development in <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"P\">P.</span> miniata</em> sea stars and <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"S\">S.</span> kowalevskii</em> acorn worms.</p>"},{"id":"47dfeffb-82c7-4a0c-9384-14f92af9a0ad","content":"<p>rather than undergoing changes resulting in the eventual elimination of a head region in their radial body plan, as previously assumed, sea stars&rsquo; morphology evolved to completely lack a trunk and consist primarily of a head region.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["47dfeffb-82c7-4a0c-9384-14f92af9a0ad"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the morphology (form and structure) of sea stars, a type of echinoderm. The text indicates that echinoderms have radially symmetrical body plans (symmetrical around a central point, usually in the form of a star), whereas most animals have bilaterally symmetrical body plans (symmetrical along an axis running from head to tail through a trunk). According to the text, sea stars are unusual echinoderms because, despite their radial body plan, they descended from known bilateral ancestors. This shift in body plan was thought to be a process of losing the genetic markers associated with the head region. The text explains that by comparing the genes of one sea star species (<em>P.</em> <em>miniata</em>) to those of a close relative, the acorn worm, researchers determined that instead, anterior (head) genes are active across the sea star&rsquo;s entire body, posterior (tail) genes are active in limited, peripheral locations of the body, and no trunk-related genes are active. This finding strongly suggests that, rather than becoming "headless" as they evolved from a bilateral ancestor, sea stars developed a body plan consisting almost entirely of a head region with a minimal tail region and no trunk region present.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t identify how any particular region of sea stars&rsquo; bodies influences the layout of sea stars&rsquo; radial symmetry. Moreover, the text indicates that the radial symmetry of echinoderms is "usually starlike," not that a starlike layout distinguishes sea stars from other echinoderms. Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the idea that sea stars evolved from an ancestor with bilateral symmetry is incorrect (describing the bilateral origin as "known") and doesn&rsquo;t address any body plans other than those with radial or bilateral symmetry. The text strongly suggests that rather than revealing something about sea stars&rsquo; origin, Formery et al.&rsquo;s findings contradict the assumption that the current body plan of sea stars is "headless." Choice C is incorrect because the text suggests that Formery et al. were able to make determinations about <em>P. miniata</em> sea stars&rsquo; body plan based on the comparability of genetic markers between <em>P. miniata </em>and <em>S. kowalevskii </em>acorn worms. The text indicates only that little or no activity was observed in certain types of genes associated with body development in <em>P. miniata</em>, not that those genes turned out to largely differ from body-development genes in <em>S. kowalevskii</em>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"086dd8cc","external_id":"350515f3-0f56-40e1-b5ce-af122f093fe5","vaultid":null,"uId":"29abf2f1-6c47-4a3a-a2ed-013c68cf4d24","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"INF","skill_desc":"Inferences","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The morphological novelty of echinoderms&mdash;marine invertebrates with radial symmetry, usually starlike, around a central point&mdash;impedes comparisons with most other animals, in which bilateral symmetry on an anterior-posterior (head to tail) axis through a trunk is typical. Particularly puzzling are sea stars, thought to have evolved a headless layout from a known bilateral origin. Applying genomic knowledge of <em>Saccoglossus kowalevskii</em> acorn worms (close relatives of sea stars, and thus expected to have similar markers for corresponding anatomical regions) to the body patterning genes of <em>Patiria miniata</em> sea stars, Laurent Formery <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"et al\">et al.</span> observed activity only in anterior genes across <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"P\">P.</span> miniata</em>&rsquo;s entire body and some posterior genes limited to the edges, suggesting that <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most logically completes the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4180cf85-c572-4011-a51e-49d8cde13e5f","content":"<p>despite the greater prevalence of anterior genes in sea stars&rsquo; genetic makeup, posterior genes active at the body&rsquo;s perimeter are primarily responsible for the starlike layout that distinguishes sea stars&rsquo; radial symmetry from that of other echinoderms.</p>"},{"id":"9d2012ba-875c-449e-a22f-ce98cd07e68d","content":"<p>contrary to the belief that they evolved from early ancestors with the bilateral form typical of many other animals, sea stars instead originated with an atypical body layout that was neither bilaterally nor radially symmetrical.</p>"},{"id":"883b1069-8080-4363-86fe-41574c1ce527","content":"<p>although the two species are closely related, there is only minimal correspondence in the genetic markers for head, tail, and trunk region development in <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"P\">P.</span> miniata</em> sea stars and <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"S\">S.</span> kowalevskii</em> acorn worms.</p>"},{"id":"47dfeffb-82c7-4a0c-9384-14f92af9a0ad","content":"<p>rather than undergoing changes resulting in the eventual elimination of a head region in their radial body plan, as previously assumed, sea stars&rsquo; morphology evolved to completely lack a trunk and consist primarily of a head region.</p>"}],"keys":["47dfeffb-82c7-4a0c-9384-14f92af9a0ad"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer because it most logically completes the text&rsquo;s discussion of the morphology (form and structure) of sea stars, a type of echinoderm. The text indicates that echinoderms have radially symmetrical body plans (symmetrical around a central point, usually in the form of a star), whereas most animals have bilaterally symmetrical body plans (symmetrical along an axis running from head to tail through a trunk). According to the text, sea stars are unusual echinoderms because, despite their radial body plan, they descended from known bilateral ancestors. This shift in body plan was thought to be a process of losing the genetic markers associated with the head region. The text explains that by comparing the genes of one sea star species (<em>P.</em> <em>miniata</em>) to those of a close relative, the acorn worm, researchers determined that instead, anterior (head) genes are active across the sea star&rsquo;s entire body, posterior (tail) genes are active in limited, peripheral locations of the body, and no trunk-related genes are active. This finding strongly suggests that, rather than becoming \"headless\" as they evolved from a bilateral ancestor, sea stars developed a body plan consisting almost entirely of a head region with a minimal tail region and no trunk region present.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t identify how any particular region of sea stars&rsquo; bodies influences the layout of sea stars&rsquo; radial symmetry. Moreover, the text indicates that the radial symmetry of echinoderms is \"usually starlike,\" not that a starlike layout distinguishes sea stars from other echinoderms. Choice B is incorrect because the text doesn&rsquo;t suggest that the idea that sea stars evolved from an ancestor with bilateral symmetry is incorrect (describing the bilateral origin as \"known\") and doesn&rsquo;t address any body plans other than those with radial or bilateral symmetry. The text strongly suggests that rather than revealing something about sea stars&rsquo; origin, Formery et al.&rsquo;s findings contradict the assumption that the current body plan of sea stars is \"headless.\" Choice C is incorrect because the text suggests that Formery et al. were able to make determinations about <em>P. miniata</em> sea stars&rsquo; body plan based on the comparability of genetic markers between <em>P. miniata </em>and <em>S. kowalevskii </em>acorn worms. The text indicates only that little or no activity was observed in certain types of genes associated with body development in <em>P. miniata</em>, not that those genes turned out to largely differ from body-development genes in <em>S. kowalevskii</em>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669205,"updateDate":1743430669205}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669205, 1743430669205),
    ($SATQ$08b28c1a$SATQ$, $SATQ$bb6e68d6-a740-46c5-bb4b-de50ba90fb20$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$cf376b08-accc-44ac-9aa9-49d20f5c95e0$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A researcher conducted an experiment inspired by studies suggesting that people may benefit from feeling frightened in certain circumstances, such as when watching scary movies or visiting haunted attractions. The researcher recruited several participants and had them walk through a local haunted house attraction. Immediately after exiting the attraction, each participant completed a survey about their experience. Based on the survey responses, the researcher claims that feeling frightened in controlled situations can boost a person&rsquo;s mood and confidence.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which quotation from a participant would best illustrate the researcher&rsquo;s claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0b02eb87-9e03-4d77-83e1-0dfc87254cd2","content":"<p>&ldquo;After I came out of the haunted house, I felt very accomplished and less stressed.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"1fad9cf3-7b39-4a9b-aa4e-43151197efe4","content":"<p>&ldquo;My friends kept laughing as we were walking through the haunted house.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"13003b7d-22f4-47f9-b0bb-d6cb31af2392","content":"<p>&ldquo;The haunted house was scary at first, but I knew everyone was just acting, so I felt less scared after a few minutes.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"3fb1936f-08ef-4990-bc62-08a54f9ded85","content":"<p>&ldquo;The sense of relief I felt at the end of the haunted house was similar to the feelings I have when I finish a scary movie.&rdquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0b02eb87-9e03-4d77-83e1-0dfc87254cd2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. This choice illustrates both &ldquo;feeling frightened in controlled situations&rdquo; (the haunted house) and the benefit of a boosted mood (&ldquo;less stressed&rdquo;) and confidence (&ldquo;very accomplished&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This choice isn&rsquo;t the best illustration of the claim. While laughing may indicate a good mood, this choice provides no evidence of &ldquo;feeling frightened&rdquo; or boosted confidence. Another choice provides better evidence. Choice C is incorrect. This choice isn&rsquo;t the best illustration of the claim. This choice demonstrates &ldquo;feeling frightened&rdquo; in a controlled environment, but it doesn&rsquo;t provide strong evidence of boosted mood or confidence. Another choice provides better evidence. Choice D is incorrect. This choice isn&rsquo;t the best illustration of the claim. While a &ldquo;sense of relief&rdquo; could be interpreted as a boosted mood, this choice doesn&rsquo;t provide direct evidence of &ldquo;feeling frightened&rdquo; or of increased confidence. This choice simply suggests that haunted houses and scary movies have a similar effect. Another choice provides better evidence for the researcher&rsquo;s claim. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TXT-E003$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$90d23879-b1f7-45ef-ba50-ad7afa3e8911$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"08b28c1a","external_id":"bb6e68d6-a740-46c5-bb4b-de50ba90fb20","vaultid":"cf376b08-accc-44ac-9aa9-49d20f5c95e0","uId":"844b337e-2d96-40f6-8588-ebad0f58a527","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A researcher conducted an experiment inspired by studies suggesting that people may benefit from feeling frightened in certain circumstances, such as when watching scary movies or visiting haunted attractions. The researcher recruited several participants and had them walk through a local haunted house attraction. Immediately after exiting the attraction, each participant completed a survey about their experience. Based on the survey responses, the researcher claims that feeling frightened in controlled situations can boost a person&rsquo;s mood and confidence.</p>","stem":"<p>Which quotation from a participant would best illustrate the researcher&rsquo;s claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0b02eb87-9e03-4d77-83e1-0dfc87254cd2","content":"<p>&ldquo;After I came out of the haunted house, I felt very accomplished and less stressed.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"1fad9cf3-7b39-4a9b-aa4e-43151197efe4","content":"<p>&ldquo;My friends kept laughing as we were walking through the haunted house.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"13003b7d-22f4-47f9-b0bb-d6cb31af2392","content":"<p>&ldquo;The haunted house was scary at first, but I knew everyone was just acting, so I felt less scared after a few minutes.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"3fb1936f-08ef-4990-bc62-08a54f9ded85","content":"<p>&ldquo;The sense of relief I felt at the end of the haunted house was similar to the feelings I have when I finish a scary movie.&rdquo;</p>"}],"keys":["0b02eb87-9e03-4d77-83e1-0dfc87254cd2"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. This choice illustrates both &ldquo;feeling frightened in controlled situations&rdquo; (the haunted house) and the benefit of a boosted mood (&ldquo;less stressed&rdquo;) and confidence (&ldquo;very accomplished&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This choice isn&rsquo;t the best illustration of the claim. While laughing may indicate a good mood, this choice provides no evidence of &ldquo;feeling frightened&rdquo; or boosted confidence. Another choice provides better evidence. Choice C is incorrect. This choice isn&rsquo;t the best illustration of the claim. This choice demonstrates &ldquo;feeling frightened&rdquo; in a controlled environment, but it doesn&rsquo;t provide strong evidence of boosted mood or confidence. Another choice provides better evidence. Choice D is incorrect. This choice isn&rsquo;t the best illustration of the claim. While a &ldquo;sense of relief&rdquo; could be interpreted as a boosted mood, this choice doesn&rsquo;t provide direct evidence of &ldquo;feeling frightened&rdquo; or of increased confidence. This choice simply suggests that haunted houses and scary movies have a similar effect. Another choice provides better evidence for the researcher&rsquo;s claim. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TXT-E003","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"90d23879-b1f7-45ef-ba50-ad7afa3e8911","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959376,"updateDate":1691007959376}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959376, 1691007959376),
    ($SATQ$08ff903e$SATQ$, $SATQ$56d2e5de-24d6-4ec7-a918-beec3ca46fe0$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$47be29f1-9413-4da5-a8dd-b0a1c0c83c87$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>A museum curator is writing a biographical statement about Trinidadian-born Chinese dancer, choreographer, and teacher Dai Ailian for a new exhibit on Chinese dance. The curator claims that some of the pieces Dai created shortly after arriving in mainland China in 1941, such as the solo dance&nbsp;<em>Yao Drum</em>, reflect a desire to represent the dances of local communities Dai visited during her travels through China.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which quotation from a work by a dance historian would be the most effective evidence for the curator to include in support of this claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2524a136-5501-4f59-b6f0-7ea92216bb30","content":"<p>&ldquo;There is no sound or music accompanying Dai&rsquo;s movements in <em>Yao Drum</em>, aside from the sounds of drumsticks beating against a drum and against each other.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"a1031cd0-91cd-4c57-81fc-dd9e83318ba4","content":"<p>&ldquo;Unlike some of the works Dai created in the early 1940s, <em>Yao Drum</em> does not feature a narrative structure, humorous elements, or references to real-life events.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"7cc27178-31ff-4ed3-895e-ed8f087ef106","content":"<p>&ldquo;<em>Yao Drum</em> was inspired by a ceremonial dance Dai witnessed during her time performing field research among the Yao people in the province of Guizhou in 1941 or 1942.&rdquo;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"1b0526ee-173c-454c-93ee-41e9c5c2eb23","content":"<p>&ldquo;<em>Yao Drum</em> is notable for its intense physicality, with Dai performing sharp jumps, swift turns, and dramatic sweeps of her legs through the air as she moves in circles on the stage.&rdquo;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7cc27178-31ff-4ed3-895e-ed8f087ef106"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. This choice clearly states that <em>Yao Drum</em> was inspired by a ceremonial dance of the Yao people. This directly supports the curator&rsquo;s claim that some of Dai&rsquo;s pieces &ldquo;represent the dances of local communities&rdquo; she visited. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice discusses the sound and music in <em>Yao Drum</em>, but it doesn&rsquo;t connect these elements to &ldquo;the dances of local communities,&rdquo; which is the focus of the claim. Choice B is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t mention the dances of local communities, but instead discusses how <em>Yao Drum</em> is different from some of Dai&rsquo;s earlier works. Choice D is incorrect. This choice describes the choreography of <em>Yao Drum</em>, but it doesn&rsquo;t connect these elements to &ldquo;the dances of local communities,&rdquo; which is the focus of the claim. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TXT-M006$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$650b7967-7484-4a5b-8bf0-4ec60c008a2d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"08ff903e","external_id":"56d2e5de-24d6-4ec7-a918-beec3ca46fe0","vaultid":"47be29f1-9413-4da5-a8dd-b0a1c0c83c87","uId":"a251caad-f9f9-49b7-848b-c8ccc7a14ffe","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>A museum curator is writing a biographical statement about Trinidadian-born Chinese dancer, choreographer, and teacher Dai Ailian for a new exhibit on Chinese dance. The curator claims that some of the pieces Dai created shortly after arriving in mainland China in 1941, such as the solo dance&nbsp;<em>Yao Drum</em>, reflect a desire to represent the dances of local communities Dai visited during her travels through China.</p>","stem":"<p>Which quotation from a work by a dance historian would be the most effective evidence for the curator to include in support of this claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2524a136-5501-4f59-b6f0-7ea92216bb30","content":"<p>&ldquo;There is no sound or music accompanying Dai&rsquo;s movements in <em>Yao Drum</em>, aside from the sounds of drumsticks beating against a drum and against each other.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"a1031cd0-91cd-4c57-81fc-dd9e83318ba4","content":"<p>&ldquo;Unlike some of the works Dai created in the early 1940s, <em>Yao Drum</em> does not feature a narrative structure, humorous elements, or references to real-life events.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"7cc27178-31ff-4ed3-895e-ed8f087ef106","content":"<p>&ldquo;<em>Yao Drum</em> was inspired by a ceremonial dance Dai witnessed during her time performing field research among the Yao people in the province of Guizhou in 1941 or 1942.&rdquo;&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"1b0526ee-173c-454c-93ee-41e9c5c2eb23","content":"<p>&ldquo;<em>Yao Drum</em> is notable for its intense physicality, with Dai performing sharp jumps, swift turns, and dramatic sweeps of her legs through the air as she moves in circles on the stage.&rdquo;</p>"}],"keys":["7cc27178-31ff-4ed3-895e-ed8f087ef106"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. This choice clearly states that <em>Yao Drum</em> was inspired by a ceremonial dance of the Yao people. This directly supports the curator&rsquo;s claim that some of Dai&rsquo;s pieces &ldquo;represent the dances of local communities&rdquo; she visited. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice discusses the sound and music in <em>Yao Drum</em>, but it doesn&rsquo;t connect these elements to &ldquo;the dances of local communities,&rdquo; which is the focus of the claim. Choice B is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t mention the dances of local communities, but instead discusses how <em>Yao Drum</em> is different from some of Dai&rsquo;s earlier works. Choice D is incorrect. This choice describes the choreography of <em>Yao Drum</em>, but it doesn&rsquo;t connect these elements to &ldquo;the dances of local communities,&rdquo; which is the focus of the claim. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TXT-M006","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"650b7967-7484-4a5b-8bf0-4ec60c008a2d","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959377,"updateDate":1691007959377}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959377, 1691007959377),
    ($SATQ$09775cbf$SATQ$, $SATQ$15287480-0c69-412a-a5ec-856eedeac05e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In a series of experiments, Julio Sevilla and Claudia Townsend showed that manipulating the space between products in store displays can influence consumers&rsquo; views of those products. Participants in several of the experiments regarded the same products in the same (generic) retail settings as significantly more valuable when the product-to-space ratio was low than when it was high. But in one of the experiments, Sevilla and Townsend arranged the same jewelry with different levels of intervening space at an upscale retailer (Tiffany &amp; Co.) and a relatively inexpensive retailer (Forever 21). The result of this experiment suggests that a store context associated with inexpensive products may moderate the effect Sevilla and Townsend observed in their other experiments.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which finding from the experiment with Tiffany &amp; Co. and Forever 21, if true, would most directly support the conclusion presented in the text?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2e10d191-283b-448a-bba1-8c6667693264","content":"<p>At Tiffany &amp; Co., participants judged jewelry spaced far apart to be substantially more valuable than jewelry spaced close together, but at Forever 21, participants judged jewelry spaced far apart to be only slightly more valuable than jewelry spaced close together.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"33ce3389-453c-4beb-ac3d-85ae1dcc1bef","content":"<p>At both Tiffany &amp; Co. and Forever 21, participants judged jewelry spaced far apart to be less valuable than jewelry spaced close together, but the difference in perceived value was significantly greater at Tiffany &amp; Co. than at Forever 21.</p>"},{"id":"5a8f584a-36c5-45af-8b96-c5a40cec4b6a","content":"<p>Participants judged jewelry spaced far apart at Tiffany &amp; Co. to be similar in value to jewelry spaced far apart at Forever 21, but participants judged jewelry spaced close together at Tiffany &amp; Co. to be more valuable than jewelry spaced close together at Forever 21.</p>"},{"id":"2a1a0850-55df-482c-bd97-d623a56fd663","content":"<p>When jewelry was spaced far apart, participants judged the jewelry at Tiffany &amp; Co. to be more valuable than the jewelry at Forever 21, but when jewelry was spaced close together, participants judged the jewelry at Tiffany &amp; Co. to be less valuable than the jewelry at Forever 21.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["2e10d191-283b-448a-bba1-8c6667693264"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would support the text&rsquo;s conclusion about the results of the experiment with Tiffany &amp; Co. and Forever 21. According to the text, Sevilla and Townsend found in several experiments that when products in a generic retail setting are displayed with a low product-to-space ratio (that is, with lots of space between them), consumers view those products as significantly more valuable than when the same products are displayed with a higher product-to-space ratio (less space between them). The text then states that the results of an experiment specifically using the contexts of an inexpensive store (Forever 21) and an upscale one (Tiffany &amp; Co.) suggest that an inexpensive store context may moderate, or lessen, that effect. If Sevilla and Townsend found that participants in the experiment with Tiffany &amp; Co. and Forever 21 judged the same jewelry items as substantially more valuable when there was lots of space between them than when there was little space between them in the upscale store (Tiffany &amp; Co.) but judged the same jewelry items as only slightly more valuable when there was lots of space between them than when there was little space between them in the inexpensive store (Forever 21), that finding would demonstrate that increased space between products was associated with less of an increase in those products&rsquo; perceived value at the inexpensive store than at the upscale store. Thus, the finding would support the text&rsquo;s conclusion that a store context associated with inexpensive goods moderates the effect Sevilla and Townsend observed in their earlier experiments.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because if Sevilla and Townsend found that at both upscale and inexpensive stores, participants judged jewelry spaced far apart to be less valuable than jewelry spaced close together, that would show the opposite of the effect the researchers observed in their earlier experiments, not show that an inexpensive store context merely moderates, or lessens, that effect. Choice C is incorrect because this finding wouldn&rsquo;t show that the effect Sevilla and Townsend observed in their initial experiments (that products spaced far apart were perceived as more valuable than the same products spaced close together) is present but lessened in inexpensive retail contexts, as the text suggests. The conclusion in the text rests on determining the difference in jewelry items&rsquo; perceived value between two spacing conditions within each store and then comparing the difference for the inexpensive store (Forever 21) to the difference for the upscale store (Tiffany &amp; Co.); a finding that compares perceptions of the jewelry items&rsquo; value between the two stores but for only one type of spacing condition at a time wouldn&rsquo;t provide information about the degree of difference between spacing conditions within each type of store context. Choice D is incorrect because this finding wouldn&rsquo;t show that the effect Sevilla and Townsend observed in their initial experiments (that products spaced far apart were perceived as more valuable than the same products spaced close together) is present but lessened in inexpensive retail contexts, as the text suggests. The conclusion in the text rests on determining the difference in jewelry items&rsquo; perceived value between two spacing conditions within each store and then comparing the difference for the inexpensive store (Forever 21) to the difference for the upscale store (Tiffany &amp; Co.); a finding that compares perceptions of the relative value of jewelry items between the two stores and for only one type of spacing condition at a time wouldn&rsquo;t provide information about the degree of difference between spacing conditions within each type of store context.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"09775cbf","external_id":"15287480-0c69-412a-a5ec-856eedeac05e","vaultid":null,"uId":"f18be053-1dfb-4d65-8b52-f13116f1be54","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In a series of experiments, Julio Sevilla and Claudia Townsend showed that manipulating the space between products in store displays can influence consumers&rsquo; views of those products. Participants in several of the experiments regarded the same products in the same (generic) retail settings as significantly more valuable when the product-to-space ratio was low than when it was high. But in one of the experiments, Sevilla and Townsend arranged the same jewelry with different levels of intervening space at an upscale retailer (Tiffany &amp; Co.) and a relatively inexpensive retailer (Forever 21). The result of this experiment suggests that a store context associated with inexpensive products may moderate the effect Sevilla and Townsend observed in their other experiments.</p>","stem":"<p>Which finding from the experiment with Tiffany &amp; Co. and Forever 21, if true, would most directly support the conclusion presented in the text?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2e10d191-283b-448a-bba1-8c6667693264","content":"<p>At Tiffany &amp; Co., participants judged jewelry spaced far apart to be substantially more valuable than jewelry spaced close together, but at Forever 21, participants judged jewelry spaced far apart to be only slightly more valuable than jewelry spaced close together.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"33ce3389-453c-4beb-ac3d-85ae1dcc1bef","content":"<p>At both Tiffany &amp; Co. and Forever 21, participants judged jewelry spaced far apart to be less valuable than jewelry spaced close together, but the difference in perceived value was significantly greater at Tiffany &amp; Co. than at Forever 21.</p>"},{"id":"5a8f584a-36c5-45af-8b96-c5a40cec4b6a","content":"<p>Participants judged jewelry spaced far apart at Tiffany &amp; Co. to be similar in value to jewelry spaced far apart at Forever 21, but participants judged jewelry spaced close together at Tiffany &amp; Co. to be more valuable than jewelry spaced close together at Forever 21.</p>"},{"id":"2a1a0850-55df-482c-bd97-d623a56fd663","content":"<p>When jewelry was spaced far apart, participants judged the jewelry at Tiffany &amp; Co. to be more valuable than the jewelry at Forever 21, but when jewelry was spaced close together, participants judged the jewelry at Tiffany &amp; Co. to be less valuable than the jewelry at Forever 21.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["2e10d191-283b-448a-bba1-8c6667693264"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would support the text&rsquo;s conclusion about the results of the experiment with Tiffany &amp; Co. and Forever 21. According to the text, Sevilla and Townsend found in several experiments that when products in a generic retail setting are displayed with a low product-to-space ratio (that is, with lots of space between them), consumers view those products as significantly more valuable than when the same products are displayed with a higher product-to-space ratio (less space between them). The text then states that the results of an experiment specifically using the contexts of an inexpensive store (Forever 21) and an upscale one (Tiffany &amp; Co.) suggest that an inexpensive store context may moderate, or lessen, that effect. If Sevilla and Townsend found that participants in the experiment with Tiffany &amp; Co. and Forever 21 judged the same jewelry items as substantially more valuable when there was lots of space between them than when there was little space between them in the upscale store (Tiffany &amp; Co.) but judged the same jewelry items as only slightly more valuable when there was lots of space between them than when there was little space between them in the inexpensive store (Forever 21), that finding would demonstrate that increased space between products was associated with less of an increase in those products&rsquo; perceived value at the inexpensive store than at the upscale store. Thus, the finding would support the text&rsquo;s conclusion that a store context associated with inexpensive goods moderates the effect Sevilla and Townsend observed in their earlier experiments.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because if Sevilla and Townsend found that at both upscale and inexpensive stores, participants judged jewelry spaced far apart to be less valuable than jewelry spaced close together, that would show the opposite of the effect the researchers observed in their earlier experiments, not show that an inexpensive store context merely moderates, or lessens, that effect. Choice C is incorrect because this finding wouldn&rsquo;t show that the effect Sevilla and Townsend observed in their initial experiments (that products spaced far apart were perceived as more valuable than the same products spaced close together) is present but lessened in inexpensive retail contexts, as the text suggests. The conclusion in the text rests on determining the difference in jewelry items&rsquo; perceived value between two spacing conditions within each store and then comparing the difference for the inexpensive store (Forever 21) to the difference for the upscale store (Tiffany &amp; Co.); a finding that compares perceptions of the jewelry items&rsquo; value between the two stores but for only one type of spacing condition at a time wouldn&rsquo;t provide information about the degree of difference between spacing conditions within each type of store context. Choice D is incorrect because this finding wouldn&rsquo;t show that the effect Sevilla and Townsend observed in their initial experiments (that products spaced far apart were perceived as more valuable than the same products spaced close together) is present but lessened in inexpensive retail contexts, as the text suggests. The conclusion in the text rests on determining the difference in jewelry items&rsquo; perceived value between two spacing conditions within each store and then comparing the difference for the inexpensive store (Forever 21) to the difference for the upscale store (Tiffany &amp; Co.); a finding that compares perceptions of the relative value of jewelry items between the two stores and for only one type of spacing condition at a time wouldn&rsquo;t provide information about the degree of difference between spacing conditions within each type of store context.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061175,"updateDate":1755117061175}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061175, 1755117061175)
